["Just as, when a man activates and illuminates his whole body, someone who said it was absurd for the man's power to be in the toe of his foot would be thought foolish, because in granting that the man extends through the whole and acts in it, he prevents him from being in the part—so the person who grants and believes that the Word of God is in the universe, and that the universe is illuminated and moved by him, should not think it absurd that one human body is also moved and illuminated by him.", "But if they think it is unseemly for us to speak of the Savior's manifestation in a human being because the human race is generated and came into being from nothing, then it is time for them to cast him out of creation as well; for creation also came into being from nothing through the Word.", "And if, even though creation is generated, it is not absurd for the Word to be in it, then it is not absurd for him to be in a human being either. For whatever they think about the whole, they must necessarily think about the part as well. For the human being, as I said before, is also a part of the whole.", "Therefore it is not at all unseemly for the Word to be in a human being, and for all things to be illuminated and moved by him and in him—as even their own writers say: 'In him we live and move and have our being'.", "What then do we say that deserves mockery, if the Word uses as an instrument for manifestation that in which he already is? For if he were not in it, he could not have used it. But if we have already granted that he is in the whole and in the parts, what is unbelievable if he also manifests himself in the things in which he is?", "For he is wholly present by his own powers in each thing and in all, and orders all things ungrudgingly. Had he wished to use a voice and make himself and his Father known through the sun or moon, through heaven or earth, through the waters or fire, no one would call it absurd, since he holds all things together at once and is present with them all and in each part, showing himself invisibly. In the same way it would not be absurd that he who orders all things and gives life to the whole, and who willed to make himself known through human beings, used a human body as an instrument to reveal the truth and the knowledge of the Father. For humanity also is a part of the whole.", "And just as the mind, being throughout the whole man, is signified by a part of the body—I mean the tongue—and no one surely says that the substance of the mind is diminished because of this, so the Word, being through all things, would not appear unseemly if he used a human instrument. For if, as I said before, it is unseemly to use a body as an instrument, it is unseemly also for him to be in the whole."]